WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:00.973
- All right, y'all.

00:00:00.973 --> 00:00:02.310
Let's move on to our first guest.

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.960
You know her from the blockbuster
"Jurassic World" movies,

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.530
as well as many others that
I love, like "The Village,"

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.710
she's the daughter of director Ron Howard,

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:11.280
and like her famous father,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.990
she recently moved behind the camera

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.040
for a documentary on dads.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.680
Let's welcome Bryce
Dallas Howard, everybody.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.660
- Hi! (laughs)

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.120
- Hello, I love you, and I'm
very excited that you're here.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.390
So where are you right now?

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.150
It looks very relaxing, you look chill.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.640
- Well, I'm in New York.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:28.473
- Oh.

00:00:28.473 --> 00:00:30.860
- And there's a, I'm in Upstate New York,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.860
and there's a backdrop,
and then behind this

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.267
is just like garage. (laughing)

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.690
- Okay, I was like that's awesome.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.060
All of us have our, this
is like my living room,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.400
and they just put a set in it, so yeah.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.080
Everything is fake.
- Oh my God.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.380
It's like even more fake
for TV, it's awesome.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.780
Well, before I forget, congratulations.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:53.630
This is really awesome.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:55.120
You're officially a college graduate.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.870
That's amazing that you went back.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:57.883
Nobody does that.

00:00:59.042 --> 00:00:59.875
- You know what I did?

00:00:59.875 --> 00:01:03.510
I kind of never really left school.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.170
And my teachers like when I
was taking a leave of absence

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.920
to start working and to
like do "The Village"

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.140
and stuff like that, I
kept saying to my teachers,

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.417
I'm like, "I'm gonna come
back, I'm gonna come back,

00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:14.730
"I'm gonna come back."

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.340
And right away, I started taking classes

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.350
at an accredited university
in order to transfer

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:22.890
to eventually graduate.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.766
I didn't think it was
gonna take me 21 years.

00:01:25.766 --> 00:01:27.650
- Yeah, well, you've been a little busy

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.630
making huge successful movies, so yeah.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.490
Not that they need to be huge
and successful, but they are.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.323
So that's okay.

00:01:34.323 --> 00:01:35.500
I just think it's awesome.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.760
My mom's a teacher, so when I told her

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.320
I wasn't going to college
and I was gonna be a waitress

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.570
living on a mattress to be a singer,

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.773
it wasn't the best thing she'd ever heard.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:47.550
She wasn't very excited about the notion,

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.533
so it worked out, luckily.

00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:50.366
- Yeah.

00:01:50.366 --> 00:01:51.199
- But how did you celebrate?

00:01:51.199 --> 00:01:52.777
'Cause so many things, like I
had my birthday in quarantine,

00:01:52.777 --> 00:01:55.140
everybody's had like
moments, anniversaries,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.360
stuff like that happening in quarantine,

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.690
how'd you celebrate graduating?

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.523
- You know what?

00:01:58.523 --> 00:02:00.220
It was really kind of amazing.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.220
Like my parents got one of those things,

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.590
one of those signs that
you can put in the grass

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.610
in front of your house that's like

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.740
congratulations, graduate
Bryce, or whatever I like.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:12.907
- Oh my gosh,

00:02:12.907 --> 00:02:13.740
that's so Southern.
- There're like parents

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.900
- that have a graduate, and I had a,

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.670
my husband sent off this
thing called like Tribute

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.070
where you can just like
record tributes for people,

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.500
and so he sent it to like
all of my friends and family

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.980
and folks that I've known
forever and ever and ever

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.070
and they all recorded
videos, just you know,

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.242
being lovely and
congratulating me and stuff.

00:02:36.242 --> 00:02:37.810
You know, all of that.
- That's really sweet!

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.115
- I had my daughter
Katie, and she was about,

00:02:40.115 --> 00:02:42.170
I don't know, 10 months
old, something like that,

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:44.550
just kinda holding her over
my head and I was smiling,

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.730
I have my mouth wide open and
she had her mouth wide open,

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.610
and then she vomited in a forceful column,

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:56.470
I guess they call it projectile
vomiting directly into,

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:57.900
like it hit my uvula.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.170
It just went right into my throat.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:04.380
And of course, your reaction
is to throw this person

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.310
as far away from you as
possible, but I didn't.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.780
I held onto her, and I
just spit as much of it

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:14.173
as I could out, and that's
when I realized I was a dad.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.250
- All right, y'all, I'm back
with Bryce Dallas Howard.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.100
That was a look at her
new documentary, "Dads,"

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.380
which is out on Apple TV+ tomorrow,

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.570
so y'all gotta check it out.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.650
But Bryce, your new
movie is a different take

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:26.870
on being a dad.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.900
Can you tell me a little
about it and who's in it?

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:30.733
- Yeah.

00:03:30.733 --> 00:03:33.310
So this is a documentary called "Dads,"

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:38.310
and it's a celebration of modern
fathering around the globe.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:43.890
So I got to interview all
different kinds of dads,

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.520
comedian dads, I have
this perspective that

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:52.530
standup comedians are our
modern-day philosophers,

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.620
and I wanted to profile,
I call them hero dads,

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.113
hero dads from around the world,

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:03.760
and that was sort of like the
combination between the two,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.440
and then during the course
of making the movie,

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.267
I kept saying, "Oh, we
need to profile a dad

00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:10.920
"who's an expecting father."

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.050
Someone who hasn't had a kid
yet and then becomes a father

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.800
during the course of the movie,
and I kept not finding it,

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.130
and I was about halfway through shooting,

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.660
and then found out that
my brother and his wife

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.530
were gonna have their first child.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.450
And so I was like, okay,
- So cool.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:25.670
I got my expecting dad.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.200
So there is an aspect of
the movie that is kind of

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.410
the Howard story of sorts.
- So you didn't plan that?

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.120
- To have your family be as big of a part

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.020
as they ended up playing?

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:36.853
- No.

00:04:36.853 --> 00:04:39.410
No, I kind of, like early on,

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:42.870
I sort of mentioned the
possibility to my dad,

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:43.930
you know, it'd be great if,

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:46.070
'cause my grandfather
had recently passed away,

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:49.330
and I had filmed an interview
with him several years prior,

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.210
and I was like well maybe I can go back

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.220
and watch some of that interview

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:53.660
and see if there's anything of value

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.460
to put in here.
- How cool!

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:56.972
But my dad was like, "No."

00:04:56.972 --> 00:04:58.146
(Bryce laughs)
(Kelly groans)

00:04:58.146 --> 00:04:59.870
- He honestly, he was so nervous about it,

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:04.820
and he just, you know, and I
think his concerns were fair.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.210
He's like if we lived for over 60 years

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.850
as a private public family,

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.291
why would we want to do that?

00:05:10.291 --> 00:05:11.540
- That's really incredibly hard.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:12.373
- Yeah.

00:05:12.373 --> 00:05:14.540
Yeah, and we've been lucky in that regard.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.200
Why peek behind the curtain now?

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.200
But with my brother being
the expecting father,

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:25.187
it just, like I said, it just made sense.

00:05:25.187 --> 00:05:28.900
And so my dad, when he
finally saw the film,

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.650
he was like, "Okay, I understand."

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:32.410
- Well, what made him a great dad?

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.370
Because this is an awesome thing,

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:35.610
'cause not all of us, I'm one of them,

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.410
I didn't grow up with a great dad,

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.930
but I love hearing about how people did,

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.470
and I have my, you know,
one of my best friends

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:44.810
is also my assistant,
and she had a great dad,

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:47.220
and my nanny has a great
dad, and I love hearing that

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.480
because it's also, you
know, something for people

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:52.580
that didn't have fathers to
kinda know how to set the bar

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.370
for what they should be
expecting for a father

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:56.070
for their children or as a husband,

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.170
or so it, but how was your dad?

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.230
What made him a great dad?

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:01.490
'Cause he had to have
been really busy, right?

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:02.544
- You know what?

00:06:02.544 --> 00:06:05.770
He was very present.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:10.060
And he figured out, my
parents together figured out,

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:13.200
given their life situation,
how to be present.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.850
And it looks different for
every single family, you know?

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:19.500
Like everyone has a lot of
plates that they're spinning,

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.690
and it's different for every individual.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:26.080
And so what that looked like
in terms of my childhood

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.120
was we were just traveling,

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.140
you know, my mom would like figure it out

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:31.560
so that all four kids,

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.540
we could go wherever my dad was going,

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.250
and we would enroll in local schools

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:37.990
or get homeschooled and--
- Wow.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:39.830
- It's almost like touring musicians.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:40.663
It's almost like how,
- Really.

00:06:40.663 --> 00:06:43.160
'cause a lot of musicians
bring their kids on the road

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:44.410
and families on the road.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.420
- Yes, that's exactly, exactly it.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.220
And it was awesome, because I loved it.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.900
And my parents figured out pretty early

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.750
that they could really control me

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:55.520
by threatening that I wouldn't
be allowed to go to set

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:56.494
if I didn't behave.

00:06:56.494 --> 00:06:57.920
(both laughing)

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.554
- I'm not above bribery
or threatening, myself.

00:07:00.554 --> 00:07:01.387
- Yes.

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:02.883
- No, I think that's cool.
- And so that happened.

00:07:02.883 --> 00:07:03.716
- [Bryce] Yeah.

00:07:03.716 --> 00:07:04.891
- Yeah!

00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:07.041
Well, I have a little surprise for you.

00:07:07.041 --> 00:07:08.058
- Oh, do you?

00:07:08.058 --> 00:07:09.864
- I have a little Father's
Day surprise for you.

00:07:09.864 --> 00:07:10.957
A video--
- A Father's Day?

00:07:10.957 --> 00:07:13.360
Yes, a video was sent to us just for you.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:14.210
Here, here we go.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:16.270
We're gonna play it.
- Oh my God!

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:17.103
- Hey, Kelly.

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:19.490
Well, let's see, an
embarrassing story about Bryce.

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:21.410
She was a great kid.

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.460
And she loved visiting the set.

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.330
And she was very, very helpful.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:28.100
In fact, by the time she was 11 or 12,

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:32.560
she was actually pitching in
as like a production assistant.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.430
In fact, she was so efficient,

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.640
that she was finally
given the responsibility

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.450
of having a walkie-talkie.

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:42.520
You know, so that the
production assistants

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.781
could talk to one another and
give direction and guidance,

00:07:45.781 --> 00:07:48.790
and I mean, that was a real honor

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:53.790
and a show of appreciation
for how mature she was.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:54.860
It was great.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:56.230
The first day, she was terrific.

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:57.500
She was on it.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.210
The second day was good, too,

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.110
except suddenly, over the walkie-talkie,

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:06.110
we heard Bryce ordering
the extras holding area

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:10.550
to be moved from one side of
a building over to the other.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.130
Now, granted, she was trying
to get it out of the sun

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:16.780
and into the shade, and her
thinking was pretty good,

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.630
but it's not quite cool
to have the 12 year old

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:21.210
taking over the production.

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:23.720
So she lost her walkie-talkie that day,

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:26.730
but I knew that one day, she'd
wanna be behind the scenes

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.513
calling the shots, and
she does it really well.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.040
- Oh my gosh, that's amazing.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:35.520
- I like to take personal responsibility.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:37.770
- I like, no, and you know what?

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.570
I know, but there's something to that,

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:41.170
even that moment as a 12 year old,

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:43.440
like I think there are
moments for everyone

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.760
that's truly successful,
there are those moments,

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:48.200
whatever that walkie-talkie
moment is for people,

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.000
but you're truly successful
'cause at the very beginning,

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.400
even if it was one person,

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.110
they told you that you were amazing,

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.480
and you could do anything you
wanted, and you believed them.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:57.750
And I think that those are the moments

00:08:57.750 --> 00:08:59.520
that you're so bold, and you're like,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.880
well, I got this, I can do this.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:01.713
Who cares that I'm 12?

00:09:01.713 --> 00:09:04.990
Like I think that that's
kind of what makes you you,

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:06.190
and what makes you special,

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:07.900
and kind of alight in what you do.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:09.280
But thank you so much, Bryce,

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:10.360
and thanks for calling in.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.153
Congratulations again on
graduating, that's super cool.

